  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">2. Home Level 2 AC vs Commercial DC Fast Charging: The Price Divide</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    One of the most critical aspects of EV ownership economics is <em>where</em> you replenish your battery. Charging costs vary dramatically depending on whether you plug in overnight at home or use high-output highway chargers:
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-6 not-prose">
    <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-emerald-50/60 border-2 border-emerald-200">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2 font-black text-emerald-950 text-sm">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-house-chimney text-emerald-600"></i>
        <span>Home Level 2 (AC Wallbox 7kW - 11kW)</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium mb-3">
        Tied directly to your residential electrical meter. Taking advantage of time-of-use (TOU) off-peak night tariffs allows you to charge a 60 kWh pack (e.g. Tesla Model 3 or BYD Atto 3) for the absolute lowest cost.
      </p>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-bold text-emerald-900 bg-white p-2.5 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
        <strong>Typical Cost:</strong> $0.10 - $0.20 per kWh / Rs 20 - 35 per unit.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 border-2 border-gray-200">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2 font-black text-gray-950 text-sm">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-bolt text-cyan-600"></i>
        <span>Commercial DC Fast (50kW - 350kW)</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium mb-3">
        Located at motorways, shopping malls, and commercial plazas. High electrical demand surcharges and network operator profit margins make commercial DC charging 2x to 3x more expensive than home charging.
      </p>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-bold text-gray-800 bg-white p-2.5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <strong>Typical Cost:</strong> $0.35 - $0.55 per kWh / Rs 70 - 110 per unit.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> For commuters who complete 80% to 90% of their charging at home overnight, the operational savings are massive. Even if you take occasional weekend road trips using public DC fast charging, your weighted average cost per kilometer remains roughly 65% to 75% lower than gasoline.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">3. Case Study: Tesla Model 3 RWD vs Toyota Camry (Annual Math)</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Let us examine a real-world scenario of an office commuter traveling <strong>15,000 kilometers (approx. 9,300 miles) annually</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 leading-relaxed">
    <li>
      <strong>Petrol Sedan (Toyota Camry / Honda Civic):</strong> Delivering an average of 12.0 km/L (28 MPG), the vehicle burns <strong>1,250 liters of fuel</strong>. At an average fuel price of $1.50 per liter (or Rs 270/L), the annual fuel bill totals <strong>$1,875 (Rs 337,500)</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tesla Model 3 RWD (60 kWh LFP):</strong> Operating at an average efficiency of 6.4 km/kWh (15.6 kWh/100 km), the vehicle consumes <strong>2,343 kWh of electricity</strong>. At an average residential tariff of $0.16 per kWh (or Rs 32/kWh), the annual charging cost totals <strong>$375 (Rs 75,000)</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Net Direct Energy Savings:</strong> Over <strong>$1,500 (Rs 262,500) preserved every single year</strong> on fuel alone, excluding savings on engine oil changes, spark plugs, and brake friction pads!
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">4. Case Study: BYD Atto 3 vs Petrol Compact Crossover</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    In the fast-growing compact crossover segment, the <strong>BYD Atto 3</strong> (equipped with BYD's ultra-durable Blade LFP battery) offers 60.5 kWh of gross capacity and approximately 420 km of WLTP city range.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Compared against a popular petrol crossover (such as a 2.0L Toyota RAV4 or Hyundai Tucson delivering 10.5 km/L), the Atto 3 requires approximately 16.5 kWh per 100 km. Over a 5-year ownership window covering 75,000 km, the petrol vehicle demands approximately 7,140 liters of fuel, while the Atto 3 consumes roughly 12,375 kWh of energy. The cumulative cash retention exceeds <strong>$7,200 to $9,000 (Rs 1,400,000+)</strong> over five years.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">5. Practical Tips to Maximize Your EV Charging Economics</h2>
  <ol class="space-y-3 mb-8 list-decimal pl-6 leading-relaxed font-medium">
    <li>
      <strong>Enroll in Time-of-Use (TOU) Night Tariffs:</strong> Program your vehicle or smart Wallbox charger to only draw power between midnight and 6:00 AM when utility grids offer their lowest off-peak rates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leverage Solar Net-Metering:</strong> If you have rooftop solar panels, scheduling weekend daytime charging turns excess solar generation directly into free vehicular range.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Precondition the Cabin While Plugged In:</strong> Cool or heat your car while it is still connected to the home charger. This utilizes grid electricity rather than draining battery capacity for initial climate control.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pre-Map Public Chargers:</strong> On long motorway journeys, prioritize high-reliability charging networks with transparent per-kWh billing rather than time-based hourly parking surcharges.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Summary Conclusion -->
  <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gray-50 border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-3xl not-prose">
    <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Final Verdict: Is an EV Truly Cheaper in 2026?</h3>
    <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
      Yes. When evaluated on empirical kilowatt-hour consumption versus gasoline liters, an electric vehicle costs between <strong>70% and 80% less to power</strong> under home charging conditions. Even with commercial DC fast chargers factored in, the total cost of ownership break-even horizon consistently occurs within 2.5 to 3.5 years for average daily commuters.
    </p>
